(5760180, 836667, 76335, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by LevT:</b></div><hr/><i> <table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by bvy:</b></div><hr/><i> Quite honestly I''m having trouble reconciling the fun, casual pose with the somberness of the place. The title doesn''t quite bridge the gap for me. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Brian, I agree that it is troubling even though perhaps only human (this is a candid shot), but that was the whole point of this photo (and the title) to show this "visual oxymoron". sorry that it did not work for you. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>So goes anonymous voting and commenting. There''s certainly no need to be sorry. I probably spent more time writing (and rewriting) this comment than any other. I almost let it go, but felt I had to say <i>something</i>. Technically, it''s a fine photo and I didn''t lowball it. But I did think long and hard about it. So if your goal was to get people thinking (as any good photograph should) then you certainly succeeded.</td>', 'Originally posted by LevT: Originally posted by bvy: Quite honestly I''m having trouble reconciling the fun, casual pose with the somberness of the place. The title doesn''t quite bridge the gap for me.   Brian, I agree that it is troubling even though perhaps only human (this is a candid shot), but that was the whole point of this photo (and the title) to show this "visual oxymoron". sorry that it did not work for you.   So goes anonymous voting and commenting. There''s certainly no need to be sorry. I probably spent more time writing (and rewriting) this comment than any other. I almost let it go, but felt I had to say something. Technically, it''s a fine photo and I didn''t lowball it. But I did think long and hard about it. So if your goal was to get people thinking (as any good photograph should) then you certainly succeeded.', '2009-12-08 16:08:45.000000', NULL, 0),
(5760183, 836706, 31664, '<td valign="top">This is worth more than the score you got.  A little dark, I had to turn up might brightness to see the 2nd person,  I think you could have bought out a little more detail without loosing the mood.  But you subject matter is fantastic and out side your usual shots.  Don''t be dishartend by the score this is good.</td>', 'This is worth more than the score you got.  A little dark, I had to turn up might brightness to see the 2nd person,  I think you could have bought out a little more detail without loosing the mood.  But you subject matter is fantastic and out side your usual shots.  Don''t be dishartend by the score this is good.', '2009-12-08 16:12:13.000000', NULL, 0),
(5760184, 836599, 68586, '<td valign="top">That is a very cool shot.</td>', 'That is a very cool shot.', '2009-12-08 16:12:24.000000', NULL, 0),
(5760190, 831846, 68504, '<td valign="top">Whoo. Photography slid way over into art: Chinese brush painting, abstract plus dream.</td>', 'Whoo. Photography slid way over into art: Chinese brush painting, abstract plus dream.', '2009-12-08 16:24:14.000000', NULL, 0),
(5760218, 836727, 111403, '<td valign="top">Though this is a DIGITAL photography site, I am surprised that no one recognized what he was doing. This hand belongs to Mike one of the "lab rats" that I work with. We work in an industrial dark room. He is using a contact printer (like an enlarger) The negative is a round shot negative, which comes from a round shot camera. This camera was used by E.O.Goldbeck! We are called lab rats because we run around in a giant pitch black (no red light when printing because we develop color) building "squeaking" to avoid running into one another. The shot was taken with no tripod and no photoshop. This is good old fashioned shooting techniques using a new age camera. Im a bit disapointed with the scores, I was hoping people would realize the skill it takes to get a shot that used nothing but the safe light coming from a contact room and a camera.. but its a digital site, I guess I should have known lol. I do appreciate the MU comment.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2009-12-08 16:47:29.</i></td>', 'Though this is a DIGITAL photography site, I am surprised that no one recognized what he was doing. This hand belongs to Mike one of the "lab rats" that I work with. We work in an industrial dark room. He is using a contact printer (like an enlarger) The negative is a round shot negative, which comes from a round shot camera. This camera was used by E.O.Goldbeck! We are called lab rats because we run around in a giant pitch black (no red light when printing because we develop color) building "squeaking" to avoid running into one another. The shot was taken with no tripod and no photoshop. This is good old fashioned shooting techniques using a new age camera. Im a bit disapointed with the scores, I was hoping people would realize the skill it takes to get a shot that used nothing but the safe light coming from a contact room and a camera.. but its a digital site, I guess I should have known lol. I do appreciate the MU comment.', '2009-12-08 16:45:15.000000', '2009-12-08 16:47:29.000000', 0),
